      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fair point — the spec is silent on the missing-resource case, so returning 405 there isn\u0027t a violation; this patch isn\u0027t closing a compliance gap. What it fixes is an inconsistency, and it\u0027s grounded in RFC 9110 itself (which DSP0266 builds on):\n\nLink: \nRFC - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-403-forbidden\nDSP - https://www.dmtf.org/sites/default/files/standards/documents/DSP0266_1.20.0.pdf\n\n\u003e RFC - In Section 15.5.4 defines 404 as the standard way to \"hide the current existence\" of a resource (used there for 403→404, but the same logic applies to any status code that would otherwise reveal existence).\n\n\u003e RFC - In Section 10.2.1 requires a 405 response to include an Allow header listing \"the target resource\u0027s currently supported methods\" — which presupposes the resource exists and has defined methods. A 405 for a URL with no resource behind it is asserting something that isn\u0027t true.\n\nSo today, for the same resource and the same caller/privileges, GET/PATCH/DELETE correctly reveal \"this doesn\u0027t exist\" (404) while POST/PUT incorrectly imply \"this exists but doesn\u0027t support that method\" (405) — a direct contradiction, not just a style inconsistency.\n\nOn \"simple code\": the router-level probe is actually simpler than what we replace — we deletes the ad-hoc per-resource methodNotAllowed() override in account_service.hpp and the trie.hpp reorder it as needed, centralizing the behavior once for every route instead of requiring each resource handler to remember to do it.",
